(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-3-fungi-of-medical-importance-quiz"]={"title":"Fungi of Medical Importance — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is tinea pedis mainly spread?","choices":["Puncture by thorns or splinters","Going barefoot as a daily habit","Exposure to public surfaces","Contact with infected animals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tinea pedis (foot) and tinea manuum (hand) are spread by exposure to public surfaces and occur between the digits and on the soles. Tellingly, tinea pedis is rare where people usually go barefoot, so it is the shared surfaces, not bare feet, that matter. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 39, 43, 44 and 45"},{"q":"Which community measure reduces inhalation of Coccidioides spores in an endemic area?","choices":["Oiling roads to keep down dust","Vaccinating outdoor workers","Isolating Valley fever patients","Clearing pigeon roosts from buildings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coccidioides arthrospores are inhaled from dust, so prevention targets dust: oiling roads, planting to minimize erosion and using dust masks. Farming, road construction or repair, archaeological digs, landslides and dust storms all raise exposure. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 19, 26, 30 and 56"},{"q":"Blastomyces dermatitidis lies dormant in warm, dry weather. When does it sporulate?","choices":["In hot, dry summer weather","After drought, rain and windstorms","Inside the host at body heat","In colder, wetter weather"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blastomyces is dormant in warm, dry weather and sporulates in the colder, wetter weather that follows, releasing the conidia that people inhale. This cycle plays out in the soils of the midwestern and southeastern United States and southern Canada. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 30 and 33 and image on Slide 6"},{"q":"Which fungi naturally inhabit the human body and can pass person to person or by fomites such as shared exercise equipment?","choices":["Histoplasma and Blastomyces","Coccidioides and Paracoccidioides","Aspergillus and Cryptococcus","Dermatophytes and Candida"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike most fungal pathogens, which need no host and are generally not communicable, dermatophytes (literally 'skin plants') and Candida naturally inhabit the human body and are transmissible from person to person or by fomites such as shared exercise equipment. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 26, 30, 33, 37, 56 and 60"},{"q":"What does Coccidioides form in the lungs after its spores are inhaled?","choices":["Fungal balls of septate hyphae","Chains of block-like arthroconidia","Spherules that release endospores","Yeasts that bud inside phagocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An inhaled arthrospore develops into a spherule (a giant sporangium) that produces endospores, which are released in the lungs; the free-living stage, by contrast, is block-like arthroconidia. Immunocompetent people usually fight the infection off, while compromised people can develop meningitis, osteomyelitis and skin granulomas. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 18, 28, 30 and 60 and image on Slide 31"},{"q":"At which temperature does a thermally dimorphic fungus grow as unicellular yeast?","choices":["20 C","25 C","30 C","37 C"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thermally dimorphic fungi grow as multicellular molds at 30 C and as unicellular yeasts at 37 C, human body temperature; in the animal habitat (35 to 40 C) they live as parasitic, budding yeasts. Culturing at room temperature and at 37 C demonstrates the switch. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5 and 17 and image on Slide 6"},{"q":"To what practice can the resistance of Aspergillus to azole fungicides be attributed?","choices":["Amphotericin B in transplant care","Agricultural crop spraying","Hospital disinfectant overuse","Long topical courses for ringworm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aspergillus is a very common airborne soil fungus, and its resistance to azole fungicides can be attributed to agricultural practices: spraying crops to prevent losses due to fungi. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 19, 20, 45, 52 and 60"},{"q":"In which setting does cutaneous candidiasis typically occur?","choices":["Nail beds under nail extensions","Chronically moist skin and burns","Gritty nodules on scalp hairs","Skin punctured by thorns or wood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous candidiasis occurs in chronically moist areas of skin and in burn patients; intertriginous lesions in the armpits, groin and under the breasts are common in obese patients. The resident yeast takes hold where skin stays damp or damaged. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 39, 45, 49, 53 and 54"},{"q":"A wrestler living in a crowded dormitory has ringworm of the groin and scrotal skin. Which name fits?","choices":["Tinea cruris","Tinea pedis","Tinea barbae","Tinea manuum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinea cruris, the jock itch, affects the groin and scrotal regions and is pervasive among athletes and in crowded living conditions. An athlete in crowded housing with groin and scrotal involvement fits it exactly. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 44 and 45"},{"q":"How does amphotericin B damage fungal cells?","choices":["It strips away the fungal capsule","It binds membrane lipids, opening leaks","It dissolves the thick fungal cell wall","It blocks the mold-to-yeast switch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amphotericin B forms complexes with fungal cell membrane lipids, causing openings through which cytoplasmic ions seep out. The action is not selective enough to spare human cells, since fungi and humans are both eukaryotes, hence its toxicity. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 14, 18 and 20"},{"q":"On what basis do most scientists now classify Pneumocystis as a fungus rather than a protozoan?","choices":["Its genetic analysis","Its response to antifungals","Its growth as hyphae","Its antibody titers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The taxonomy of Pneumocystis is still debated, fungus or protozoan, but most scientists lean toward a fungal classification because of genetic analysis. Its usual drugs do not settle the question: one is an antiprotozoal and the other an antibacterial. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 58 and image on Slide 59"},{"q":"A dye-stained sputum specimen shows a mass of branched septate hyphae and no yeast cells. Which fungus does this fit?","choices":["Aspergillus species","Candida albicans","Cryptococcus neoformans","Coccidioides immitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Branched septate hyphae in a sputum specimen are the picture of Aspergillus, an airborne soil fungus whose spores germinate in the lungs and can form fungal balls. Dyes amplify these fungal elements in tissue specimens. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 30, 55, 57 and 60 and image on Slide 18"},{"q":"A yeast isolate incubated in serum for 2 to 4 hours grows short hyphal sprouts. Which identification test is this?","choices":["Hair infection test","Test for dimorphism","Periodic acid-Schiff stain","Germ tube test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Some yeasts, when incubated in serum for 2 to 4 hours, sprout tiny hyphal tubes called germ tubes. The germ tube test sits among the identification tests for isolates from blood, cerebrospinal fluid, pus and vaginal secretions, alongside differential media and biochemical and antigen tests. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, image on Slide 17"},{"q":"A negative stain of a specimen shows encapsulated budding yeast. Which organism does this demonstrate?","choices":["Candida albicans","Aspergillus fumigatus","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A negative stain demonstrating encapsulated budding yeast is the picture of Cryptococcus neoformans, the widespread encapsulated yeast of pigeon-roost soil. Biochemical and serological testing are the best diagnostic tools for it. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 17, 18, 55, 56, 57 and 58 and image on Slide 59"},{"q":"Antibiotic therapy and catheters predispose patients to which opportunistic fungus?","choices":["Aspergillus species","Cryptococcus species","Fusarium species","Candida species"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Among the common opportunistic fungi, Candida is the one associated with antibiotic therapy and catheters, alongside diabetes, corticosteroids and immunosuppression. It is the dominant opportunistic pathogen and its infections are usually endogenous. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 51, 52 and 64 and image on Slide 9"},{"q":"A fungal infection involves the stratum corneum and occasionally the upper dermis, but no deeper. What level of invasion is this?","choices":["Cutaneous","Superficial","Subcutaneous","Systemic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous involvement takes in the stratum corneum and occasionally the upper dermis. It is deeper than the extremely shallow epidermal colonization of superficial infection but stops short of the subcutaneous tissue reached after a puncture wound. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 14 and image on Slide 23"},{"q":"Which treatment is used for systemic sporotrichosis?","choices":["Pentamidine and/or co-trimoxazole","Topical azoles and debridement","Amphotericin B and/or itraconazole","Heat packs and/or potassium iodide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systemic sporotrichosis is treated with amphotericin B and/or itraconazole. Heat packs and/or super-saturated potassium iodide are the treatment given for infection of the appendages and lungs; once the disease is systemic, the drug pair is used. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 39, 45 and 58"},{"q":"How can Fusarium reach and infect the eye?","choices":["Eating grain contaminated by it","Through contaminated contact lenses","From close person-to-person contact","Spread from the nose in diabetes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fusarium species, a mainly tropical soil mold, occasionally infect the eyes, for example through contaminated contact lenses, as well as toenails, feet and burned skin, and can result in severe disability and death. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 63, 64 and 65"},{"q":"Which description fits paracoccidioidomycosis?","choices":["Rarest primary mycosis, usually mild","Commonest primary mycosis, usually mild","Rarest primary mycosis, usually systemic","An opportunist needing impaired defenses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Paracoccidioidomycosis, endemic in Central and South America (mainly the cool, humid soils of Brazil, Colombia, Venezuela, Argentina and Paraguay), is the rarest of the primary mycoses; systemic disease is not common, and infections are usually mild and self-limiting. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 7, 26 and 37 and image on Slide 13"},{"q":"Sporothrix schenckii is a saprobic fungus. What does saprobic mean?","choices":["It switches from mold to yeast at 37 C","It lives as normal flora on human skin","It feeds on dead or decaying matter","It must live on a living host"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A saprobe derives its nutrition from dead or decaying matter. Sporothrix schenckii is a very common saprobic fungus that decomposes plant matter in soil, which is why thorns, wood, bare roots, pine straw and hay carry it to people. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 12, 39 and 53"},{"q":"A fungus with few, if any, virulence factors causes a systemic infection in a patient taking long-term corticosteroids. How is this mycosis best classified?","choices":["Dermatophytosis","Opportunistic mycosis","True (primary) mycosis","Subcutaneous mycosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Few, if any, virulence factors plus a host weakened by corticosteroids is the picture of an opportunistic mycosis: these fungi usually start infection in immunocompromised hosts, and predisposing factors such as primary illness, corticosteroid use, diabetes and cancer should be considered. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 7, 14, 22 and 42"},{"q":"Which traits make hospital outbreaks of Candida auris hard to eradicate?","choices":["Drug, heat and disinfectant resistance","A capsule shown by negative stain","Arthroconidia spread by windstorms","Switching between mold and yeast forms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Candida auris is emerging as a serious cause of invasive candidiasis, with hospital outbreaks that are difficult to eradicate: it shows antifungal drug resistance, is thermoresistant and is partially resistant to common disinfectants. It was first identified in an ear infection, hence its name. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 14, 30, 52, 56 and 57"},{"q":"Which factor may lead to white or black piedra?","choices":["Handling infected household animals","Inhaling spores from bird guano","Poor hygiene, such as oily hair products","Close contact with an infected person"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both piedras are considered non-contagious and may result from poor personal hygiene, such as oily hair products and tight hats or veils. They are superficial mycoses of the hair shaft, and neither is common in the United States. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 12, 26, 44, 49 and 50"},{"q":"How do mycotoxicoses usually arise?","choices":["Ingesting or inhaling fungal toxins","Fungi invading through broken skin","Spores provoking a hypersensitivity","Overgrowth of resident yeast flora"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mycotoxicoses are usually caused by ingesting or inhaling fungal toxins. Examples are aflatoxin on grains, corn and peanuts and ergot on rye or other grains contaminated by Claviceps fungi; they are intoxications, not infections. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 38, 52 and 65"},{"q":"Into what form do inhaled Blastomyces conidia convert in the lungs?","choices":["Block-like arthroconidia","Multiplying yeast cells","Spherules full of endospores","Branched septate hyphae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blastomyces is a dimorphic fungus, free-living in soil; its inhaled conidia convert to yeasts that multiply in the lungs, the yeast phase that dimorphic fungi adopt at body temperature in an animal host. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 6, 18, 30 and 33 and image on Slide 34"},{"q":"Which ability defines a true (primary) fungal pathogen?","choices":["Spreading readily from person to person","Staying a mold at human body temperature","Invading and growing in a healthy host","Needing impaired host defenses to infect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A true or primary fungal pathogen can invade and grow in a healthy host. Its most striking adaptation is the switch from hyphal cells to yeast cells with the change to host temperature. An opportunist, by contrast, has little or no virulence of its own and needs impaired host defenses. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 5, 7 and 12"},{"q":"Where does Histoplasma capsulatum grow in nature?","choices":["Cool, humid soils of South America","Decaying plants, pine straw and hay","Moist, nitrogen-rich soil with guano","Alkaline, salty soil of hot, dry land"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Histoplasma capsulatum grows in moist soil with a high nitrogen content, especially soil containing bird and bat guano. When wind or digging disturbs that soil, the conidia become airborne and are inhaled. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 26, 30, 37 and 39 and image on Slide 28"},{"q":"Why is immunization typically ineffective for preventing fungal infections?","choices":["Antifungal drugs already prevent infection","Reinfection is exceedingly common","Fungi cause too few human infections","Fungi turn into yeasts inside the host"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immunization is typically ineffective against fungal infections because reinfection is exceedingly common. Control therefore relies on antifungal drugs (amphotericin B, flucytosine, azoles and nystatin), surgical removal of damaged tissue where needed, and limiting contact with spores. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 4, 5, 7, 12 and 19"},{"q":"Which drugs are common treatment options for histoplasmosis?","choices":["Heat packs and potassium iodide","Amphotericin B and azoles","Pentamidine and co-trimoxazole","Topical azoles for several weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amphotericin B and azoles are common treatment options for histoplasmosis, the Ohio Valley fever caught by inhaling conidia from soil rich in bird or bat guano. Amphotericin B is the drug of choice for many fungal infections. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 26, 39, 45 and 58"},{"q":"On the World Health Organization's fungal priority list, which fungus is ranked one tier above Coccidioides?","choices":["Paracoccidioides species","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Cryptococcus gattii","Histoplasma species"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Coccidioides is in the medium priority group; Histoplasma species are in the high priority group, one tier above, with other Candida species, Fusarium, the eumycetoma agents and the Mucorales. The critical group sits above both. — PAJ5200.Fungi of Medical importance.pptx, Slide 11, 56 and 58"}]};
